WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.588 --> 00:00:12.551
I want you to imagine it’s 1992,

00:00:12.551 --> 00:00:15.637
and you're talking to a telecoms expert.

00:00:16.179 --> 00:00:21.184
She tells you that mobile phone
text messaging is going to fundamentally

00:00:21.226 --> 00:00:24.312
change the way we communicate.

00:00:24.771 --> 00:00:26.398
You think,

00:00:26.439 --> 00:00:30.861
"But we use our phones
just for voice calls.

00:00:30.861 --> 00:00:34.197
Why on Earth am I going to go from this

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:38.410
to tapping on a screen
with my fingers and my thumbs?"

00:00:38.994 --> 00:00:43.707
In 1992, the first text message was sent.

00:00:43.748 --> 00:00:46.543
After a slow start,

00:00:46.543 --> 00:00:50.213
text-based services exploded.

00:00:50.589 --> 00:00:54.509
This paved the way
for the smartphone revolution

00:00:54.551 --> 00:00:56.803
that we have seen today.

00:00:57.721 --> 00:01:04.227
And other technology trends
have followed that same curve.

00:01:05.103 --> 00:01:09.816
I know how much we struggle to comprehend

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:14.905
and predict what
“exponential change” means.

00:01:15.989 --> 00:01:21.411
Because I was at Nokia in 1993

00:01:21.453 --> 00:01:24.789
when that technological
revolution started.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.170
And now as head of UN Climate Change,

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:36.718
I'm here to tell you that what was true
for mobile phones back in 1992,

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.055
and other revolutionary shifts,

00:01:40.055 --> 00:01:44.434
is also true for climate action today.

00:01:45.101 --> 00:01:51.483
To make climate action
go from linear to exponential pace,

00:01:51.524 --> 00:01:55.111
it's not just my job to make it happen.

00:01:55.528 --> 00:01:58.406
It's also your job, too.

00:01:58.949 --> 00:02:05.830
We all know that climate impacts
are hitting harder and harder every day.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:11.503
The ambition we need to see,
reducing emissions,

00:02:11.544 --> 00:02:13.713
adapting to impacts

00:02:13.713 --> 00:02:18.885
and providing the means
to address the crisis,

00:02:18.885 --> 00:02:21.262
are severely lacking.

00:02:22.013 --> 00:02:24.808
But just like technology,

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:29.312
public opinion has its tipping point.

00:02:29.938 --> 00:02:33.817
In my youth, I campaigned
against apartheid.

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:37.988
Participating in that fight,

00:02:37.988 --> 00:02:40.865
those demonstrations,

00:02:40.907 --> 00:02:47.580
meant shaking the foundations of a system
many thought would never change.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.334
But then, one day,

00:02:51.334 --> 00:02:57.132
after generations of slow progress,
the system changed.

00:02:57.841 --> 00:03:01.011
The pressure was too great.

00:03:01.386 --> 00:03:03.596
The point tipped.

00:03:05.056 --> 00:03:10.812
We're here in Detroit,
the heart of car manufacturing.

00:03:11.354 --> 00:03:13.690
Take a look at this graph.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:18.903
Electric vehicle sales
are expected to increase

00:03:18.945 --> 00:03:23.116
to become 70 percent by 2030.

00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:25.577
Another example.

00:03:25.618 --> 00:03:27.454
Solar energy.

00:03:27.454 --> 00:03:31.124
Between 2010 and 2020,

00:03:31.166 --> 00:03:37.297
we moved from 20 gigawatts
of solar energy installed

00:03:37.297 --> 00:03:39.883
to 150 gigawatts.

00:03:40.842 --> 00:03:43.970
Now, just three years later,

00:03:44.012 --> 00:03:49.642
that number is expected to increase
to 350 gigawatts per year.

00:03:50.101 --> 00:03:52.437
And by 2030,

00:03:52.479 --> 00:03:59.194
that number is expected to increase
again to 1,000 gigawatts per year.

00:03:59.903 --> 00:04:03.031
Change can come fast.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.785
But time isn't on our side.

00:04:07.619 --> 00:04:11.539
We have less than seven years
between now and 2030

00:04:11.539 --> 00:04:15.210
to halve our global emissions.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.340
So the question is: Will those tipping
points come soon enough

00:04:20.381 --> 00:04:24.594
to avoid the catastrophic impacts
of climate change?

00:04:25.345 --> 00:04:30.975
At out current speed,
change will come too late

00:04:30.975 --> 00:04:35.522
to limit warming to 1.5 degrees Celsius.

00:04:36.106 --> 00:04:40.360
That is the hard truth
of the global stocktake

00:04:40.401 --> 00:04:45.115
that will be completed
by the UNFCCC this year.

00:04:45.156 --> 00:04:48.076
But this is where you come in.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:54.332
You may think it's not
your responsibility to drive change.

00:04:55.208 --> 00:04:59.712
You may not think you have
the power to make this happen.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.050
Both of those statements are wrong.

00:05:05.135 --> 00:05:06.886
At the UNFCCC,

00:05:06.928 --> 00:05:10.348
we are the custodians of the process.

00:05:10.723 --> 00:05:13.935
We're the defenders of the agreements.

00:05:13.935 --> 00:05:18.148
We are the champions of parties
and the agreements that they make.

00:05:19.232 --> 00:05:20.984
But globally,

00:05:20.984 --> 00:05:27.740
we're collectively in a state
of what the author Kim Stanley Robinson

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.535
describes as “The Dithering.”

00:05:31.703 --> 00:05:34.539
A state of indecision

00:05:34.581 --> 00:05:40.461
and a state of inaction
in the face of a global crisis.

00:05:41.588 --> 00:05:45.633
But countries will agree
to boost in action.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:51.347
If enough people encourage them to do so.

00:05:51.973 --> 00:05:57.520
Contact your representatives and ask them
what they are doing to deliver.

00:05:58.479 --> 00:06:00.648
What plans do they have

00:06:00.648 --> 00:06:05.987
to ensure that your concerns
on climate count?

00:06:06.362 --> 00:06:09.782
That direct action does work.

00:06:10.408 --> 00:06:15.788
I have seen positions change
time and time again

00:06:15.788 --> 00:06:19.542
when citizens engage directly.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.630
Every time you buy something,

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.840
ask yourself the question:

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.301
Is this the greenest option?

00:06:28.801 --> 00:06:33.806
Ask those around you
about their green choices.

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:36.142
Learn new things.

00:06:36.184 --> 00:06:39.479
Educate and mobilize.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:45.985
The power of human organization
can result in change.

00:06:47.028 --> 00:06:49.530
Which brings me to my final point.

00:06:50.073 --> 00:06:53.326
You don't have to be a politician

00:06:53.368 --> 00:06:56.120
or a prominent business person

00:06:56.162 --> 00:06:59.082
to make a significant contribution.

00:06:59.624 --> 00:07:03.503
Climate action calls for all skill sets.

00:07:04.671 --> 00:07:06.673
Ask yourself:

00:07:06.714 --> 00:07:09.133
What am I passionate about?

00:07:10.093 --> 00:07:12.345
What am I good at?

00:07:13.137 --> 00:07:15.306
What am I training for?

00:07:15.682 --> 00:07:17.809
Then ask yourself the question,

00:07:17.809 --> 00:07:21.479
how can I apply this
to the climate crisis?

00:07:21.896 --> 00:07:25.984
And do exactly that thing.

00:07:27.235 --> 00:07:29.654
If you act,

00:07:29.654 --> 00:07:36.160
the exponential change
that is needed will happen.

00:07:36.786 --> 00:07:38.121
I thank you.

00:07:38.162 --> 00:07:42.917
(Applause)

